  "slug": "disease-associated-dysbiosis-c485",
  "summary": "Multisite and stool-plasma profiling in Parkinson cohorts links gut microbial dysbiosis to diagnosis, severity and cognitive impairment.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread in this set is the use of microbial community measurements to characterize Parkinson disease. A preprint profiling stool and plasma biomarkers in 211 Parkinson patients and 204 controls reports microbiome and dysbiosis differences tied to diagnosis and clinical severity. A second preprint profiling five body sites in 150 Parkinson's participants reports weakening cross-site microbial coordination and reduced core taxa across the cognitive impairment spectrum. The new contribution is multisite resolution, extending microbial readouts beyond a single sampling site. Other items address different questions: a multiplexed assay of more than 10,400 GBA1 variants linking glucocerebrosidase function to Gaucher and Parkinson disease, somatic mutation trajectories in ALS motor cortex neurons, multi-omics integration in mouse hippocampus and colon, and ancestral variant analysis. These do not share the microbial focus.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
